@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-json-pointer-resolution-semantics-for-ambiguous-rfc-6901-cases
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Pointer resolution semantics for ambiguous RFC 6901 cases"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6901 defines JSON Pointer syntax precisely, but leaves some resolution cases
+implementation-defined for a read/"get" operation:
+
+- The `-` array token is valid syntax (RFC 6901 §4) referring to the nonexistent member after the
+  last array element. The RFC defines it for use by JSON Patch "add", not for reading an existing
+  value.
+- The RFC doesn't mandate a runtime type for the `pointer` argument itself; that's a boundary
+  concern for this library's API, not the wire format.
+
+Because `resolvePointer` is the foundation other upcoming features will call, its failure modes
+need to be decided once and reused consistently.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `resolvePointer` always returns a value or throws; it never returns `undefined` for a
+  non-resolving pointer, so callers can't mistake "not found" for a legitimate `undefined` value
+  stored in the document.
+- The `-` array token throws (same "does not resolve" error as an out-of-range index), since there
+  is no element there to read.
+- Array index tokens are validated against `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$`; leading zeros (e.g. `01`) throw
+  rather than being parsed leniently, matching the RFC's ABNF for `array-index`.
+- A non-string `pointer` argument throws a `TypeError` immediately, per the code-quality directive
+  to validate external input at system boundaries, rather than coercing or failing deeper in the
+  traversal with a less obvious error.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers that want JSON-Patch-style "append" semantics (using `-`) will need a different function;
+  `resolvePointer` is read-only resolution.
+- Error messages are `Error`/`TypeError` instances with descriptive messages, not a typed error
+  hierarchy; if later features (e.g. a `setPointer`/patch feature) need to distinguish error kinds
+  programmatically, that will need its own decision when it comes up.
